|> Section
    title=Definition

    Since we will be talking about convergence of the distribution of random variables to the normal distribution, it makes sense to develop the general theory of convergence of distributions to a limiting distribution.

    |> Definition

        Let $(F_n)_{n=1}^\infty$ be a sequence of distribution functions. We say that $F_n$ $\textbf{converges to a limiting distribution function}$ $F$, and denote this by $F_n \implies F$, if $F_n(x)\to F(x)$ as $n\to\infty$ for any $x\in\R$ which is a continuity point of $F$. If $X, (X_n)_{n=1}^\infty$ are random variables, we say that $X_n$ $\textbf{converges in distribution to } X$ (or, interchangeably, $\textbf{converges in distribution to } F_X$) if $F_{X_n}\implies F_X$.

    This definition, which may seem unnatural at first sight, will become more reasonable after we prove the following lemma.

    |> Lemma

        The following are equivalent:

        |> ol
            class=list
            data-list-style=decimal
            |> li
                $X_n \implies X$.
            |> li
                $\mathbf{E} f(X_n) \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} \mathbf{E} f(X)$ for any bounded continuous function $f:\R\to\R$.
            |> li
                There exists a r.v. $Y$ and a sequence $(Y_n)_{n=1}^\infty$ of r.v.'s, all defined on some probability space $(\Omega, {\cal F}, \mathrm{P})$ such that $Y_n \to Y$ a.s., $Y$ is equal in distribution to $X$, and each $Y_n$ is equal in distribution to the respective $X_n$.

    |> Proof

        Proof that $2\implies 1$: Assume that $\mathbf{E} f(X_n) \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} \mathbf{E} f(X)$ for any bounded continuous function $f:\R\to\R$, and fix $x\in \R$.
        For any $t\in \R$ and $\epsilon>0$, define a function
        $g_{t,\epsilon}:\R\to\R$ by
        $$ g_{t,\epsilon}(u) = \begin{cases}
        1 & u<t, \\
        \frac{t-u+\epsilon}{\epsilon} & u \le t \le t+\epsilon, \\
        0 & u>t+\epsilon.
        \end{cases}
        $$
        Then we have that
        $$ \mathbf{E} (g_{x-\epsilon,\epsilon}(X_n)) \le F_{X_n}(x) = \mathbf{E}(\mathbf{1}_{(-\infty,x]}(X_n)) \le \mathbf{E}(g_{x,\epsilon}(X_n))$$
        Letting $n\to\infty$ gives the chain of inequalities
        $$ F_{X}(x-\epsilon) \le \mathbf{E}(g_{x-\epsilon,x}(X)) \le \liminf_{n\to\infty} F_{X_n}(x) \le \limsup_{n\to\infty}
        F_{X_n}(x) \le \mathbf{E}(g_{x,\epsilon}(X)) \le F_X(x+\epsilon). $$
        Now if $x$ is a point of continuity of $F_X$, letting $\epsilon \downarrow 0$ gives that $\lim_{n\to\infty}F_{X_n}(x) = F_X(x)$.

        Proof that $3\implies 2$: this follows immediately by applying the bounded convergence theorem to the sequence $g(Y_n)$.

        Proof that $1 \implies 3$: Take $(\Omega,{\cal F},\mathrm{P}) = ((0,1),{\cal B}(0,1), \textrm{Leb})$. For each $n\ge 1$, let $Y_n(x) = \sup\{ y : F_{X_n}(y) < x \}$ be the $\textbf{lower quantile function}$ of $X_n$, as discussed in a previous lecture, and similarly let $Y(x)=\sup\{ y : F_X(y)<x\}$ be the lower quantile function of $X$. Then as we previously showed, we have $F_Y \equiv F_X$ and $F_{Y_n}\equiv F_{X_n}$ for all $n$. It remains to show that $Y_n(x)\to Y(x)$ for almost all $x\in(0,1)$. In fact, we show that this is true for all but a countable set of $x$'s. Denote $Y^*(x) = \inf\{ y : F_X(y)>x\}$ (the $\textbf{upper quantile function}$ of $X$). As we have seen, we always have $Y(x) \le Y^*(x)$, and $Y(x) = Y^*(x)$ for all $x\in(0,1)$ except on a countable set of $x$'s (the exceptional $x$'s correspond to intervals where $F_X$ is constant; these intervals are disjoint and each one contains a rational point).

        Let $x\in(0,1)$ be such that $Y(x)=Y^*(x)$. This means that for any $y<Y(x)$ we have $F_X(y)<x$, and for any $z>Y(x)$ we have $F_X(z)>x$. Now, take a $y<Y(x)$ which is a continuity point of $F_X$. Then $F_{X_n}(y)\to F_X(y)$ as $n\to\infty$, so also $F_{X_n}(y)< x$ for sufficiently large $n$, which means (by the definition of $Y_n$) that $Y_n(x)\ge y$ for such large $n$. This establishes that $\liminf_{n\to\infty} Y_n(x)\ge y$, and therefore that $\liminf_{n\to\infty} Y_n(x)\ge Y(x)$, since we have continuity points $y<Y(x)$ that are arbitrarily close to $Y(x)$.

        Similarly, take a $z>Y(x)$ which is a continuity point of $F_X$. Then $F_{X_n}(z)\to F_x(z)$ as $n\to\infty$, so also $F_{X_n}(z)>x$ for large $n$, which implies that $Y_n(x)\le z$. Again, by taking continuity points $z>Y(x)$ that are arbitrarily close to $Y(x)$ we get that $\limsup_{n\to\infty} Y_n(x) \le Y(x)$. Combining these last two results shows that $Y_n(x)\to Y(x)$ which was what we wanted.
